at has given rise to grave concerns  about society's readiness to provide for  rising numbers of elderly people  whose pensions need to be financed by an  ever-shrinking workforce  the problem is already well known in  japan where one in four people  are over the age of 65.  experts say previous pandemics like the  spanish flu in 1918  provide only limited clues about what to  expect this time  the global birth rate did fall in 1919  but this was followed by a baby boom the  following year  usually attributed to the end of world  war one  it's far from certain whether a similar  boom could follow the coronavirus  the pace of an economic recovery and the  search for a vaccine are both likely to  play a role  but demographic trends are one thing and  personal stories  another holding a healthy baby girl in  her  arms for maria alverez memories of  giving birth during a pandemic will  forever be laced  with both tragedy and hope let's talk  about making babies with  natalie nietzsche a fertility researcher  at germany's max planck institute  natalie will birth rates really fall  because a lot of people have had  a lot of time on their hands during  lockdown  yeah i know i read the media reports as  well and while i'm sure  there will be some lockdown babies born  that as a major trend to affect  future fertility rates is extremely  unlikely and  i would say yes birth rates are going to  fall  at least in the next few years or in the  next year  let me tell you a bit more about why i  think yeah  i was just going to say we're talking  globally i guess uh are  are there differences between poorer and  richer nations  most likely we will see differences uh i  was just about to talk about the higher  income countries for example europe  north america  australia in those countries we are  seeing  the lockdowns we are seeing recessions  and we also know from previous research  that  pandemics and recessions cause fertility  to fall at least for a while so some  studies have 